# Mutation cases for W3C SCXML Appendix D — in a document with `<parallel>`,
# `executeMicrostep` owns exit, transition content and entry, and nothing above
# it may repeat any of the three.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/parallel_microstep_owns_exit_and_entry.cases`.
#
# The defect restored below was live in this tree on 2026-08-13 and produced no
# build error, no warning and no failing test. What it produced was a
# `<parallel>` in which one region kept its root and lost its leaf: the region
# still read as present in the configuration while holding no atomic state, so
# nothing in it could ever fire again. Silent, because every W3C parallel
# fixture asks its question within a single microstep, and a region without a
# leaf answers the first event correctly — it is the SECOND event that finds
# nothing there.
#
# Sibling of `parallel_region_transition_domain.cases`. That file guards the
# three Appendix D algorithms (`findLCCA`, region lookup, configuration
# reflection); this one guards the execution path that calls them. Both were
# needed: the algorithms were measured correct while the caller was wrong, so a
# case in that file says nothing about this site.
#
# A case that SURVIVES here means the gate that replaced the defect is
# decorative.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^IntegrationTests$'
mutation_targets sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h
# The three suites the round reds, measured: `AiLoopAotTest.TheBudgetEndsThe
# RunFromWhereverTheCycleIs`, `ParallelCurrentStateAotTest.SettlingCurrentState
# DoesNotDisturbTheOtherRegion` and `ParallelSelfTransitionKeepsItsLeafAotTest.
# TheSelfTransitionedRegionAnswersTheNextEvent`. Any one of them weakening is
# enough to matter, so all three are named.
mutation_oracles tests/integration/AiLoopAotTest.cpp
mutation_oracles tests/integration/ParallelCurrentStateAotTest.cpp
mutation_oracles tests/integration/ParallelSelfTransitionKeepsItsLeafAotTest.cpp

# ── Exit and entry applied twice ───────────────────────────────────
#
# §scxml-D-microstepProcedure runs exit → transition content → entry as one
# atomic step, and for a parallel policy `processTransition` has already
# completed all three before `executeTransition` regains control. Adding a
# caller-supplied exit/entry on top reads as symmetry with the non-parallel
# branch above it and is a second application: `executeExitActions` removes its
# argument from the active configuration, so re-exiting `oldState` after the
# microstep has already re-entered it takes that state back out. It is not put
# back, because `currentState_` names the last transition the microstep
# processed, which belongs to a DIFFERENT region.
#
# The trigger needs all three of: one event enabling a transition in two
# regions, one of those being a self-transition, and the two regions resting at
# different depths. Drop any one and the configuration survives — which is why
# the symmetric, equal-depth fixtures in the W3C corpus never sampled it.
#
# ⚠ The edit inserts INTO the existing parallel branch rather than creating one.
# An earlier revision appended `} else { ... }` to the `if constexpr` above,
# which compiled when it was written and stopped compiling the moment
# `e819e013e8` gave that `if` an `else` of its own — two `else` clauses on one
# `if`. The harness reported INCONCLUSIVE rather than SURVIVED, which is the
# whole reason it distinguishes them, but nothing ran this file between the two
# commits, so the guard sat unproven for a day. A mutation edit that anchors on
# the shape of neighbouring code is a claim about that shape, and this one now
# anchors on the branch it is actually restoring the defect into.

mutation_case "a parallel transition exits and enters a second time" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """        else {
            resolveCurrentStateToLeaf();
        }""",
     """        else {
            executeOnExit(oldState, preTransitionStates);
            executeOnEntry(currentState_);
            resolveCurrentStateToLeaf();
        }""")
PY
